Kent Paul Dolan schrieb:
> 
> One really good way to differentiate the classes
> would be to make the probability tables for item,
> dungeon feature, and monster generation dependent on
> class and/or race of the PC.

Two objections:
Consistency: the PCs share the same dungeon (as witnessed by
bonefiles), so there's no reason why the normal dungeon should be
equipped differently for a knight than for a rogue. But consistency
can always be fudged or ignored to fit gameplay objectives, so that's
a secondary matter.

Gameplay: shifting _some_ probabilities could be interesting, but i'd
want a closer look: improving the chances for armour and weapon is a
_net loss_ for a character. It's worst in the case of weapons, because
for standard weaponry the average character does _not_ depend on
random generation, but on monster equipment, starting equipment and
altar gifts. In the case of armour, the most desired suits are never
randomly generated, so it boils down to a slightly better chance of
obtaining magical armour pieces; and you don't need a lot of them and
they don't wear out.
I'd say that improved and shifted chances really matter in the case of
scrolls, potions, wands, rings, amulets, books and tools. For all
other items, changed chances of random generation don't matter much
(but reduced chances for vegan food can be very annoying). 
So i think the best bet would be to adjust the relative chances of the
mentioned seven item classes against one another - more scrolls at the
cost of fewer tools etc. 
Boxes and chests would have to be exempt from adjusted chances for
tools, though; and the average number of items per chest or per
complete floor level should _not_ change.

The idea has its merits, but i'm not yet convinced it would
substantially add to the game.

